  "findings": [
    "Selected write throughput versus main ranges from -0.16% to +0.40% across one/eight workers and disjoint/shared modes. All four pointwise 95% intervals exclude a 3% throughput loss; the lowest endpoint is -2.02%.",
    "Selected versus original mean throughput improves in every case, from +0.74% to +1.09%. Some intervals include small losses, so do not claim every small improvement is conclusive.",
    "Single-worker retired instructions match expected hit-path changes exactly: +3 versus main and -1 versus original. Eight-worker hardware counters were disabled; no eight-worker instruction claim is made.",
    "All protected page counters and complements match all successful worker updates, including warmup. Every process has exactly 32 dirty pages after verification, zero errors, zero measured disk I/O and zero major faults. Disjoint and single-worker shared cases have zero explicit failed claims.",
    "Eight-worker shared mode exercises contention: mean explicit failed claims per completed update are main0.08342, original0.08395 and selected0.08451. Time includes waiting inside blocking cache_get, but those internal waits are not separately counted.",
    "The source/runner/build/object/library hashes, CPU/TID assignment, selected working set, process logs, mirrored blocks, excluded preconditions, and machine-lock chronology all passed the auditor.",
    "All72 measured processes had whole-process thermal-counter increases; none was excluded and those flags cannot identify whether throttling occurred in the measured phase.",
    "These results do not reproduce a substantial resident cache-write slowdown. They do not prove complete database insertion neutrality or assign the earlier variable database wall-time difference to a cause.",
    "Disjoint/shared per-worker working sets differ. Compare profiles within each mode rather than treating their absolute rates as a pure contention contrast. All timing measures repeated mutation of already-dirty resident pages, without allocation, traversal, splits, WAL, eviction or writeback."
  ],
